### What is a palindrome check?

A palindrome check determines whether a string reads the same forward and backward. For practical purposes, the check ignores case and non-alphanumeric characters. The string "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama" is a palindrome because after removing spaces, commas, and the colon, and ignoring case, it reads "amanaplanacanalpanama" which is symmetric.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

public class PalindromeChecker {
    public static boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
        if (s == null) return false;
        int left = 0;
        int right = s.length() - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            while (left < right && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(left))) left++;
            while (left < right && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(right))) right--;
            if (Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(left)) != Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(right))) {
                return false;
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

### Why does this matter in banking?

The National Bank of India runs a rewards promotion where customers can pick vanity account nicknames. Palindromic nicknames get bonus points. Rajesh validates each submission by checking whether the nickname reads the same both ways, ignoring spaces and case, running on every submission with no extra allocation.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.validation.PalindromeChecker;

public class NicknameService {
    public boolean isEligibleForBonus(String nickname) {
        boolean palindrome = PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome(nickname);
        System.out.println("Nickname '" + nickname + "' is palindrome: " + palindrome);
        return palindrome;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NicknameService service = new NicknameService();
        service.isEligibleForBonus("Racecar");
        service.isEligibleForBonus("Savings");
        service.isEligibleForBonus("A Santa at NASA");
    }
}
```

```text
Nickname 'Racecar' is palindrome: true
Nickname 'Savings' is palindrome: false
Nickname 'A Santa at NASA' is palindrome: true
```

### How does the two-pointer technique work step by step?

Start with `left` at index 0 and `right` at the last index. In each iteration, advance `left` forward past any non-alphanumeric characters, and advance `right` backward past any non-alphanumeric characters. Now both pointers sit on meaningful characters. Compare them case-insensitively. If they differ, return false. If they match, move both pointers inward by one and repeat until they meet.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

public class StepByStepDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "A,b,B,a";
        int left = 0, right = s.length() - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            while (left < right && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(left))) left++;
            while (left < right && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(right))) right--;
            char lc = Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(left));
            char rc = Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(right));
            System.out.println("Compare s[" + left + "]='" + lc + "' with s[" + right + "]='" + rc + "'");
            if (lc != rc) { System.out.println("Mismatch!"); return; }
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        System.out.println("Palindrome!");
    }
}
```

```text
Compare s[0]='a' with s[7]='a'
Compare s[2]='b' with s[5]='b'
Palindrome!
```

### How is this different from the naive approach?

The naive approach cleans the string first by removing non-alphanumeric characters and lowercasing, then reverses it and compares. That works but allocates two extra strings. The two-pointer approach avoids all allocation by comparing in place, skipping unwanted characters as it goes.

| Aspect | Naive (clean + reverse) | Two-pointer (in-place) |
|---|---|---|
| Extra space | O(n) for cleaned string | O(1) |
| Time | O(n) | O(n) |
| Allocations | 2 new strings | None |
| Readability | Simpler | Slightly more complex |

```java
package com.bank.validation;

public class NaiveVsTwoPointer {
    // Naive approach: allocates extra strings
    public static boolean naivePalindrome(String s) {
        String cleaned = s.replaceAll("[^a-zA-Z0-9]", "").toLowerCase();
        return cleaned.equals(new StringBuilder(cleaned).reverse().toString());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String test = "A Santa at NASA";
        System.out.println("Naive:       " + naivePalindrome(test));
        System.out.println("Two-pointer: " + PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome(test));
    }
}
```

```text
Naive:       true
Two-pointer: true
```

### What are the edge cases?

An empty string is a palindrome because there are no characters to contradict symmetry. A single character is always a palindrome. A string of only punctuation like ",.!" is a palindrome because after skipping all non-alphanumeric characters, there is nothing left to mismatch. Null input should return false by convention.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

public class EdgeCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome(""));      // true
        System.out.println(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome("a"));     // true
        System.out.println(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome(",.!"));   // true
        System.out.println(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome(null));    // false
        System.out.println(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome("ab"));    // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
true
false
false
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most dangerous mistake is forgetting the `left < right` guard inside the inner skip loops. Without it, a string of only punctuation causes the pointers to run past each other, resulting in an index-out-of-bounds exception. The second mistake is comparing characters without lowercasing, which makes "Racecar" fail despite being a valid palindrome.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

public class CommonMistakesDemo {
    // WRONG: no guard in skip loop, no case folding
    public static boolean buggyPalindrome(String s) {
        int l = 0, r = s.length() - 1;
        while (l < r) {
            while (!Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(l))) l++; // may go out of bounds
            while (!Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(r))) r--; // may go out of bounds
            if (s.charAt(l) != s.charAt(r)) return false;        // case-sensitive (bug)
            l++; r--;
        }
        return true;
    }

    // CORRECT: guarded skip loops and case folding
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // buggyPalindrome(",.!") would throw StringIndexOutOfBoundsException
        System.out.println(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome(",.!"));     // true (safe)
        System.out.println(PalindromeChecker.isPalindrome("Racecar")); // true (case-insensitive)
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
```

### What about Unicode and locale issues?

`Character.toLowerCase()` works for ASCII but full Unicode case folding can be locale-sensitive. For example, the Turkish dotless i has different uppercase behavior. For bank nicknames restricted to ASCII letters and digits, this is not a concern. For internationalized input, a locale-aware normalization would be needed.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

import java.util.Locale;

public class LocaleDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        char lower = 'i';
        System.out.println("Default upper: " + Character.toUpperCase(lower));
        System.out.println("Turkish upper: " + String.valueOf(lower).toUpperCase(new Locale("tr")));
    }
}
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Time is O(n) because each pointer traverses the string at most once. Space is O(1) because no extra data structures or strings are created. The early return on the first mismatch makes typical non-palindromes fast because they often fail near the ends.

